# Tool Access Gate

## Task readiness

The secrets access owner's `preflight-secrets.mjs` is the shared decision entry
point. Before claim, the bounded resolver reads the complete live body/comments,
acceptance, proof and delivery needs; enumerates credential availability separately
from scoped capability/identity; and persists the complete vendor context after
the normal git-ignore and EAS checks. Only this small local intake metadata write
may precede claim. Normal source, branch, claim and business writes may not.

Before a context exists, fixed Implement/Track/tracker-read/secrets-access owner
loads and configured issue/comment/relationship reads remain available. The
bounded native `Agent` route uses name `input-resolver`, type `general-purpose`,
description `Resolve Lisa input`, and the exact prompt `Invoke lisa-track <ref>;
resolve the complete live issue, comments and graph; prepare requirements and
prove access before any claim. Do not implement or change source.` Only the
configured canonical ref is substituted. Other agents, skills and mutations
are not preclaim exceptions. Never create a fake complete empty header to load
the resolver or read its inputs.

Supply a complete values-free `required_access` array and
`requirements_complete: true` as JSON on stdin to the owner's
`--prepare --ref <canonical-ref>` mode. For the first context, include the complete
unedited `context_body` on that same stdin; it creates
only the fixed ignored0600 context after Git-ignore and EAS checks. Existing
context cannot be replaced through `context_body`. This is the bounded initial
resolver metadata transaction, not a general Write or shell exception.
For the native Claude `Write` tool, pass the complete canonical context as
`{file_path: "<absolute project root>/.lisa/work-item-context.md", content: "<leading requirements header plus full body>"}`.
Use the header schema above with current root/ref/config/surface/body/requirements
digests; no stored success field or credential value belongs in that header.
The before hook treats this exact-path request as data for the existing validated
atomic0600 metadata owner. It DENIES the redundant raw Write with
"Context prepared; run readiness", while explicitly performing only that bounded
metadata side effect. Run readiness next; a denial is not a fake successful tool
result. Source writes, alternate paths, symlinks, conflicting refs/config/body,
missing ignore/EAS proof and malformed headers still refuse. Existing body bytes
cannot be replaced by this adapter. Never put the complete private body in a Bash
command, heredoc, argv or Lisa-owned log; native tool payloads may still be retained
in runtime transcripts. No universal transcript privacy is claimed.
Codex retains native `tool_name: apply_patch`; its `tool_input.command` is patch
data. Only ONE whole `*** Add File: <absolute canonical context path>` operation
is decoded into the same strict metadata owner. Every content line starts with
`+`; Add File reconstructs those lines with LF and a final LF. The before hook
prepares0600 context and DENIES the redundant raw patch with the same recovery.
Relative/normalized/symlink aliases, mixed operations, update/delete/move/hunks,
bad framing/trailing text and bad headers/body/ref/config/binding still refuse.
Ordinary non-context source patches are NOT metadata: they reach normal readiness,
proceed only when qualified and remain withheld when required access is unproved.
This is a protocol/wrapper component, not execution of every native app/version.

Real SessionStart (including same-ID resume), UserPromptSubmit and SessionEnd
rotate the existing temporary root/session epoch before config/header/provider
evaluation, including subagent scopes. Early affected-action failures invalidate
the epoch before reporting denial. Restoring exact config bytes cannot revive
an old proof. SessionStart remains advisory; this reset grants no access.
The owner prepends the strictly leading
`<!-- lisa-required-access:v1` header and preserves every following body/comment
byte. Then run `--task --ref <canonical-ref>` before claim and act on its semantic
outcome. `ESTABLISHED` exits0 and proves only the exercised scope. `DENIED` exits2
and stops the affected action. `NATIVE_OWNER_REQUIRED` exits3: the documented
native owner must perform its actual immediate identity/resource probe before
the claim or affected continuation. It is not shell PASS or a definitive access
failure; do not repeat an unobservable check forever. A complete empty array checks routing and declared requirements;
it proves no other integration. Never use credential-only for an integration
whose identity/resource/operation must be proved. Never supply values, executable
paths, commands, argv, arbitrary URLs, JSON programs or successful attestations.

The header binds root, canonical ref/provider, body, effective config/provenance,
surface and requirement digests. The owner rereads them and the canonical binding
at every affected action. Missing, conflicting, incomplete, malformed or stale
metadata refuses; no cached pass survives a changed task/config/surface/body.
Only fixed owner adapters probe documented operations. GitHub's native keyring
route checks the configured repository, and proves only that GitHub operation.
Supported injected access needs no vault tool, bootstrap or health proof.
Routing credential names are capability proxies: a complete task capability for
the same routed vendor may establish access through its documented native owner.
An explicit `secrets.require` or credential-only task assertion remains independent
and must be satisfied before any handoff. An omitted routed capability still
checks its routing floor; an unrelated capability cannot satisfy that floor.

Native PreToolUse adapters call this same owner and withhold ordinary DENIED and
NATIVE_OWNER_REQUIRED actions; no-context/no-binding is not a mutation grant.
SessionStart stays advisory. Only fixed orientation/recovery, owner loading and
the expected documented read-only probes remain available while pending.
Fixed orientation reads remain available.
Required source errors use fixed safe diagnoses and recovery actions; never
reflect requirement identifiers/resources/paths, parse errors or provider output.
Do not block unrelated tasks or require optional integrations universally.

The exact readiness owner command (`node <owner>/preflight-secrets.mjs --task
--ref <canonical-ref>`, or `--prepare` with that ref and JSON on terminal stdin)
remains available during refusal. It grants no work: task mode still reports its
real verdict, and preparation only refreshes the ignored requirements metadata.
Only the exact native context Write/Add File forms above carry private metadata.
General shell chaining, redirects and unrelated commands are not exceptions.

### Native connector representation gap

The reference native owner has one narrow Notion self/fetch before/after bridge.
It uses runtime call/session/input and approved connection provenance, stable self
identity, and a recognized positive exact-resource MCP output. Arbitrary text,
empty success, supplied status, unsolicited/replayed results and wrong identity
cannot mint proof. Every pending scope must be covered, and the next identical
ordinary action atomically consumes the complete temporary observation once.
State contains safe indexes/hashes, not arguments, private IDs, result text or
credential values. Changed root/task/config/context/surface/action, failures and
intervening work invalidate it; no durable PASS or new binding schema exists.

Several runtimes have after-tool events but lack qualified response/provenance
forwarding here. Standard Notion output without the supported typed positive
contract, ChatGPT's different fetch shape, and missing native call/provenance
fields are `native-response-unrepresented`, not vendor/tool/credential absence.
Ordinary work remains withheld. The documented
`lisa-notion-access`, `lisa-linear-access` and `lisa-atlassian-access` native routes
remain valid when tier1 is genuinely unavailable; their actual immediate
identity/resource probe and guarded write/readback own the affected native
operation. A supplied passed status, transcript or shell absence proves nothing.
Capability credential names are primary candidates, not independent mandatory
secrets that can prevent a documented fallback. Explicit config assertions and
routing-floor obligations remain mandatory with their existing exceptions.
Supported primary resolution precedes eligible fallback; wrong identity cannot
be hidden by connector handoff. Provider health and capability proof stay separate.
Only the represented event/response contract is machine-enforced; fixture or
callback results do not establish native-agent execution. Otherwise report the
precise response mapping gap and retain the ordinary-action refusal and existing
owner's immediate probe/write/readback duty. A mismatched identity never authorizes
a fallback. Provider health and capability proof remain distinct observations.

Descriptors can name integrations beyond the executable adapters. Existing
documented native owners remain authoritative. Without such a native route,
unsupported executable observation is DENIED (adapter-unsupported), not an
invented connector handoff or malformed metadata. A documented cheapest
authenticated read still needs an implemented supported adapter. No header supplies
executable code, URLs, argv or attestations. Credential diagnoses use bounded
schema-validated public keys and generated requirement indexes, never raw
requirement IDs/resources or a fixture-name allowlist.
The bounded GitGuardian `token-read` adapter resolves GITGUARDIAN_API_KEY through
the same sources, reads the fixed API token-self endpoint, and compares its
workspace to the optional task-specific `gitguardian.workspaceId` anchor. It
proves that scoped token read, not a scan or vault health. API authority:
[GitGuardian API](https://api.gitguardian.com/docs). Projects not needing this
operation do not need this anchor or credential.
GitHub reads its repository/permissions separately from the authenticated user.
Native keyring success avoids a vault lookup. When native authentication is
unavailable, the same supported env/file/provider ladder supplies GH_TOKEN only
to the scoped gh child's in-memory environment, clearing conflicting aliases.
Wrong native identity never silently falls back. API failure is not vault outage.
An observed actor ID comes only from `/user`, never a repository viewer field;
repository access does not imply the actor owns that repository. Installation
tokens without `/user` prove repository scope only, with actor explicitly unobserved.

Claude, Codex, Cursor, Copilot and Devin receive the shared native before-tool
hook; OpenCode throws from tool.execute.before. Antigravity's run_command adapter
can cancel shell work; it has no SessionStart. Equivalent file-edit/native-MCP
response interception is not implemented by this adapter; the generator contract
does list PostToolUse support, so this is not a claim that agy lacks that event.
Generated artifact parity does not establish native
execution on any platform. Preserve the existing access ordering and hold/leaf/
scope/idempotency guards. Stop/report independently of tracker write success.

- If you can't reach something you need, such as a repository, a secret, an API, or a connector, say exactly what's missing in your first message and stop. Don't substitute, mock, or guess.

If the missing access is discovered after work begins, say exactly what's
missing in your next message and stop.

A flow may only take on work it can actually finish. If completing a work item —
including its empirical verification — requires an external tool or system the
agent cannot access, the flow must **tell the user exactly what access is missing and stop**, never
work around it. This is the flow-side arm of the factory
contract: intake validates that the factory has "the tooling *and provable
access to that tooling*"; this gate re-proves that promise at execution time and
enforces it for tools discovered mid-flow.

"Tool" means any external surface the work depends on, for example:

- Cloud/provider CLIs and APIs: AWS (CloudWatch logs, S3, …), GCP, Azure
- Design sources: Figma
- Bug/session capture: Jam
- Observability: Sentry, PostHog, CloudWatch
- Quality gates: SonarCloud
- Trackers and docs: JIRA/Confluence, Linear, Notion, GitHub
- Device/browser harnesses: Playwright, Maestro, Detox, simulators/emulators
- Databases, deploy targets, and protected environments

## When the gate runs

1. **Preflight** — after the completion condition is defined and before any
   implementation task starts, enumerate every tool required by (a) the
   implementation itself, (b) the proof command / verification plan, and
   (c) remote verification and post-deploy checks. Sources for the enumeration:
   the work item (description, comments, attachments — a Figma link or Jam
   capture implies that tool), acceptance criteria, `testing_requirements`,
   the `verification` metadata, and the deploy pipeline for the target
   environment.
2. **Continuously** — the moment a previously unknown tool requirement surfaces
   mid-flow (e.g. verification turns out to need CloudWatch log capture), probe
   it right then. If access is unavailable, report it and stop. Otherwise, record
   the new tool + probe result in the same places the
   preflight wrote to (the plan/tracker artifact and the affected tasks'
   `metadata.required_access`) before continuing. Discovery timing changes
   nothing about the protocol.

## Proving access

Access is proven by a **cheap, read-only probe that actually exercises the
authenticated surface** — tool presence on PATH is not access.

- Vendors with an access layer MUST be probed through their `*-access` skill
  (see the `integration-access-layer` rule): `lisa-atlassian-access`,
  `lisa-notion-access`, `lisa-linear-access`, `lisa-jam-access`,
  `lisa-sonarcloud-access`, `lisa-sentry-access`, `lisa-posthog-access`,
  `lisa-expo:play-store-access`. A loud access-skill failure naming a missing
  env var IS a failed probe.
- Vendors without an access layer are probed with the cheapest authenticated
  read the runtime offers (an MCP tool call, a CLI read, a REST GET).

Example probes:

| Tool | Probe |
|---|---|
| AWS CLI | `aws sts get-caller-identity`, plus the service-level read the task needs (e.g. `aws logs describe-log-groups --max-items 1`) |
| GitHub | a repository-scoped read against the target repo (e.g. `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo> --jq .full_name`, or the exact read the work item needs) — `gh auth status` alone only proves host auth, not access to the repository |
| Figma | a read call against the linked file via the available Figma MCP/API surface |
| Sentry / Jam / SonarCloud / PostHog / Atlassian / Linear / Notion | the matching `*-access` skill's resolve/auth check |
| Database | the project's documented read-only connection check |
| Deploy target | reach the target environment with the credentials the verify step will use |
| Device/browser harness | the harness's own doctor/smoke entry (e.g. `playwright --version` plus a trivial headless launch) |

Resolve credentials through the documented sources before probing: project
e2e config/fixtures, `.lisa.config.local.json` and environment variables, then
documented work-item credentials (e.g. `Sign-in Required`). If the documented
access path or probe cannot provide the required access, report the gap and
stop. Do not continue exploring substitute sources after confirming the gap.

Record successful probes in the flow's plan/tracker artifact
(and task `metadata.required_access` where the flow's task contract carries
it), so the verifier can confirm the gate ran.

## On failure: break out, never work around

When required access is unavailable, tell the user and stop. Use the first
message if the gap is already known, or the next message if it is discovered
mid-task. The message must state:

- the exact resource that cannot be reached and the operation it blocks;
- the observed failure and, if known, the credential name, role, permission, or
  invitation needed — never secret values, and never a guessed diagnosis;
- the read-only probe that must pass before work can resume.

Do not substitute, mock, guess, or continue other tasks as a workaround.
Reporting does not depend on access to a tracker, and it does not require
creating a new ticket. Resume after the required access is available and its
probe passes.

### Forbidden workarounds

None of the following ever substitutes for missing access:

- Swapping the verification for a weaker one the agent *can* run (unit tests or
  code reading instead of the required log capture / UI observation).
- Mocking, stubbing, or simulating the inaccessible system to make the proof
  "pass".
- Marking work done on artifact-only evidence, or asserting success without the
  runtime evidence the completion condition demands.
- Guessing at the tool's contents (e.g. implementing a design without reading
  the linked Figma file, or "fixing" a bug without the Jam/Sentry evidence the
  ticket points at).
- Silently narrowing scope so the inaccessible part is "out of scope".

Keep results already obtained, but stop further work until the required access
is available. An inaccessible tracker does not prevent reporting the blocker
to the user. Do not claim blocked acceptance criteria have passed.
